# Why study C first?

Rust becomes more interesting when ownership is not treated as arbitrary compiler ceremony. It is a way of making classes of resource-lifetime mistakes harder to express.

```rust
let s = String::from("axiom");
let t = s;
// println!("{s}"); // use after move
```

The future curriculum will compare this with explicit ownership conventions in C, rather than presenting Rust in isolation.

```callout
{
  "tone": "note",
  "title": "Comparative method",
  "body": "C will often expose the failure mode first. Rust will then show a language-level strategy for ruling out or structuring that failure mode."
}
```
